Use the attached reference image as the canonical model for the creature named "{{ vibemon.identity.name }}".

TASK
- Render ONE square 1:1 sprite contact sheet.
- The sheet contains exactly nine complete full-body copies of the same creature from the reference image.
- Arrange the nine copies in a strict three-by-three layout: top-left, top-center, top-right, middle-left, middle-center, middle-right, bottom-left, bottom-center, bottom-right.
- This is a contact sheet, not a shared scene. Each copy is independent and centered in its own equal area.
- Leave generous plain background space between copies so every sprite can be cropped cleanly.

REFERENCE LOCK
- The attached image wins over all text for anatomy, silhouette, proportions, palette, markings, appendage count, and signature feature placement.
- Do not redesign, simplify, evolve, age, accessorize, or reinterpret the creature.
- R1C2 is the closest match to the reference design. Every other cell changes only view angle, posture, gesture, and facial expression.
- Keep every attached feature rooted in the same place in every cell: tail, fins, shell, plates, crest, horns, wings, limbs, markings, and any other signature feature.
- If a pose would require adding, removing, enlarging, shrinking, splitting, or transforming a feature, keep the feature unchanged and make the pose subtler.

CANVAS RULES
- Background: flat matte {{ vibemon.aesthetic.background_color }} as one uniform wash filling every non-creature pixel.
- No scenery, terrain, horizon, sky, floor line, ground shadow, cast shadow, aura, glow, weather, particles, texture, pattern, gradient, vignette, film grain, or noise.
- No panel borders, frames, dividers, grid lines, labels, numbers, text, UI, watermarks, or visible guide marks.
- Empty matte spacing between sprites is allowed and desired. Drawn separators are not allowed.
- The only foreground marks are the nine creature bodies themselves.

COUNT AND FIT
- Treat the canvas as nine equal cells: R1C1, R1C2, R1C3, R2C1, R2C2, R2C3, R3C1, R3C2, R3C3.
- Each cell contains exactly one complete creature copy.
- No cell may be blank.
- Do not merge two cells into one large pose.
- Do not split one creature across cells.
- If the reference creature is wide, long-tailed, winged, coiled, bulky, or highly ornamented, draw every copy smaller so all nine complete bodies fit with margin.

STYLE LOCK
- Match the reference image's Pokémon Sugimori style: clean tobacco-brown lineart, warm desaturated fills, flat cel shading, gentle cream highlights, and warm shadows.
- Keep the reference palette. Do not introduce new body colors.
- Highlights stay inside the creature silhouette only.
- Do not draw detached elemental effects, speed lines, expression symbols, or decorative marks.

EMOTION RULES
- Show every emotion only through eyes, mouth, brow, posture, head angle, and the creature's existing appendages.
- Do not use punctuation or cartoon shorthand: no question marks, exclamation points, hearts, stars, sweat drops, tear drops, anger marks, thought bubbles, motion lines, or floating symbols.
- If the creature does not have arms or hands, do not invent them. Use its actual fins, claws, wings, shell, head, body mass, tail, tentacles, or other existing anatomy.

POSES
R1C1 — Back battle view. Rear view from behind, oriented toward canvas right. Face hidden or barely visible. Same anatomy as reference.
R1C2 — Hero showcase. Front or slight three-quarter view, battle-ready, focused expression. Closest match to the reference design.
R1C3 — Opponent battle view. Three-quarter confrontation pose, weight forward, intent gaze, same creature and same features.

R2C1 — Resting. Settled low, seated, crouched, perched, or compactly lying down. Calm eyes or closed eyes. No tail loop unless the reference already has that exact loop.
R2C2 — Happy. Open, playful, energetic posture using existing appendages. Bright face and smile if the mouth design allows it.
R2C3 — Frustrated or determined. Tense posture, furrowed brow, defiant tilt. Existing forelimbs or body mass drawn inward if anatomically possible.

R3C1 — Proud. Chin or head lifted, stable balanced posture, confident eyes. Composed rather than puffed-up.
R3C2 — Confused. Head tilted, uneven brows, uncertain mouth, slightly off-center weight. An existing appendage may lift toward the face if natural.
R3C3 — Sad. Lowered head, downturned mouth, heavy eyelids, gently slumped posture. No tears or floating marks.

FINAL CHECK
- Exactly nine complete creature bodies.
- One body centered in each cell.
- Same creature, same anatomy, same colors, same signature features in every cell.
- Plain matte background only.
- No symbols, text, dividers, shadows, effects, or extra marks.
